p.src:
The next operation is contraction. Identify one upper index with one lower index and sum: $T^{m}{}_{m}
  =
  T^{1}{}_{1}+T^{2}{}_{2}+\cdots.$ No free index remains, so the result should be a scalar. The transformation law verifies it: $\begin{aligned}
  \bigl(T^{m}{}_{m}\bigr)_{(y)}
  &=
  \frac{\partial y^{m}}{\partial x^{r}}
  \frac{\partial x^{s}}{\partial y^{m}}
  \bigl(T^{r}{}_{s}\bigr)_{(x)}
  \nonumber\\
  &=
  \delta^{s}{}_{r}\,T^{r}{}_{s}{}^{(x)}
  =
  \bigl(T^{r}{}_{r}\bigr)_{(x)}.
  \end{aligned}$ The paired Jacobians are inverse matrices. Their product is the identity, represented by the Kronecker delta.

