p.src:
Even flat Euclidean space has many Cartesian frames. They may be translated or rotated relative to one another. In two dimensions, $ds^{2}
  =
  (dx^{1})^{2}+(dx^{2})^{2}
  =
  \delta_{mn}\,dx^{m}dx^{n}.$ Transformations between Cartesian frames preserve this Pythagorean form. In that restricted setting the identity metric makes upper and lower components numerically equal.

